# Literature Map

## Full-Information Delayed Online Learning

- Joulani, Gyorgy, and Szepesvari (ICML/PMLR 2013) is the primary black-box delayed-feedback reduction. Verified source states the ICML 2013/PMLR venue and frames delay effects on regret; use this for the finite-grid delayed-learning step, after explicitly mapping public price observations to delayed full-information reward vectors.
- Joulani, Gyorgy, and Szepesvari (AAAI 2016), "Delay-Tolerant Online Convex Optimization," is verified. The source states a black-box reduction from delayed-feedback to standard online learning for full-information OCO and adaptive-gradient algorithms. Useful for horizon-free/adaptive variants of the grid reduction.
- Quanrud and Khashabi (NeurIPS/NIPS 2015) gives adversarial-delay regret in terms of total delay \(D\), with source page reporting \(O(\sqrt D)\)-type control. This supports bounded-delay scaling via \(D\lesssim TH\), but still needs model mapping.
- Weinberger and Ordentlich (IEEE TIT 2002) is an older fixed-delay prediction reference, now verified through DBLP/DOI metadata. Useful background for individual-sequence prediction with delayed observations; not specific to market-making fills.
- Van der Hoeven and Cesa-Bianchi (AISTATS/PMLR 2022) treats arm-dependent delays in nonstochastic bandits and experts. Verified source says the full-information experts setting has first-order bounds depending on delays/losses of the best arm. Relevant because quote delays are arm-dependent.

## Unrestricted, Skipping, And Aggregate Delay Bounds

- Thune, Cesa-Bianchi, and Seldin (NeurIPS 2019) handles nonstochastic bandits with unbounded delays and skipping excessively delayed observations; verified source gives bounds involving total delay and skip/oracle quantities. Useful for replacing a hard \(H\) truncation by adaptive skipping.
- Zimmert and Seldin (AISTATS 2020/arXiv:1910.06054) gives optimal arbitrary-delay adversarial bandit bounds with refined excluded-round sets. Verified through arXiv; relevance is conceptual for skip-set tail penalties.
- Lancewicki, Segal, Koren, and Mansour (ICML/PMLR 2021) studies stochastic bandits with unrestricted and infinite delays, with quantile-based delay dependence. Verified source; relevant for quantile alternatives to uniform tails.
- Zierahn, van der Hoeven, Lancewicki, Rosenberg, and Cesa-Bianchi (JMLR 2025) gives a modern unified analysis for delayed nonstochastic bandit feedback. Verified source; useful terminology but less direct because the truncated market-making reduction is full-information.
- Bistritz, Zhou, Chen, Bambos, and Blanchet (NeurIPS 2019) studies delayed adversarial bandit feedback and missing feedback beyond horizon. Verified source; useful for horizon-censoring distinctions, not direct proof support.
- Bistritz, Zhou, Chen, Bambos, and Blanchet (JMLR 2022), "No Weighted-Regret Learning in Adversarial Bandits with Delays," is verified. It studies delayed adversarial bandits with weighted-regret objectives and bounds involving total delay; relevant background for horizon-weighted finite-prefix dual certificates, not a delayed-fill characterization theorem.
- Gyorgy and Joulani (ICML/PMLR 2021) is now verified. It gives adversarial bandit bounds adapting to observed delays/data and includes skip-set bounds. Relevant for replacing a fixed truncation horizon by adaptive skipping; not directly full-information market making.
- Flaspohler et al. (ICML/PMLR 2021) is now verified. It reduces delayed online learning to optimism and may help treat predictable delayed fills as hints rather than worst-case lost mass.
- Esposito, Masoudian, Qiu, van der Hoeven, Cesa-Bianchi, and Seldin (ICML/PMLR 2023) is verified. It studies delayed bandits with intermediate observations; relevant as an analogue for public price history that may make some delayed fills predictable.
- Ryabchenko, Attias, and Roy (arXiv 2026) is verified as a recent reduction from delayed to immediate OCO with total-delay/outstanding-observation terms. Treat as a current candidate for adaptive truncation ideas, not as an established market-making characterization.
- Masoudian, Zimmert, and Seldin (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2024) is verified. Its abstract explicitly frames the complexity of delayed bandits in terms of information missing at decision time, measured by outstanding observations, rather than elapsed delay. This is conceptually well aligned with replacing raw \(\beta\)-tails by missing-information or skipped-feedback quantities.
- Schlisselberg, Lancewicki, Auer, and Mansour (NeurIPS 2025/arXiv:2505.24193) is verified. It gives best-of-both-worlds delayed-bandit bounds with terms involving cumulative delay and maximum missing observations. Useful for adaptive-delay context, but not direct support for the public-price full-information market-making reduction.
- Ryabchenko, Attias, and Roy (COLT/PMLR 2025) is verified. It studies capacity-constrained delayed online learning and reports full-information bounds with total-delay/tracking-capacity terms. Relevant to adaptive truncation and bounded outstanding-fill bookkeeping.
- Masoudian, Zimmert, and Seldin (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2022/arXiv:2206.14906) is verified as the earlier best-of-both-worlds delayed-bandit paper. It includes arbitrary-delay bounds using total delay, maximal delay, and outstanding observations, and is the predecessor to the 2024 robustness-to-excessive-delays work already in the map.
- Ryabchenko, Attias, and Roy (arXiv:2606.11711, 2026), "Capacity-Constrained Online Convex Optimization with Delayed Feedback," is verified. It extends capacity-constrained delayed learning to OCO/BCO and delayed-weighted reductions. Relevant to adaptive truncation, tracking-capacity, and outstanding-information sufficiency, not to raw-tail necessity.

## Market Making, Pricing, And Threshold Geometry

- Cesa-Bianchi, Cesari, Colomboni, Foscari, and Pathak, "Market Making without Regret" (COLT/PMLR 2025), verified. Adjacent market-making regret model; useful for terminology and relation to pricing/auction structure, but not the same delayed-fill model.
- Abernethy and Kale, "Adaptive Market Making via Online Learning" (NeurIPS 2013), verified from the NeurIPS PDF. It assumes exogenous price series and online learning over market-making strategies; relevant background for exogenous-price modeling.
- Duetting, Guruganesh, Schneider, and Wang (ICML/PMLR 2023) is now verified. It studies one-sided Lipschitz maximization, motivated by pricing/contract design, and gives very small regret in that setting. Potential tool for threshold reward geometry and improved discretization, but delayed/adversarial time variation may block direct application.
- Kleinberg and Leighton (FOCS 2003) is verified through DOI/DBLP metadata and accessible technical-report text. It is foundational posted-price regret background and gives lower-bound context for threshold/pricing geometry.
- Zhang, Lin, Zheng, Feng, Teng, and Deng (ICLR/OpenReview 2024) is verified. It studies learning thresholds with latent values and censored feedback, including one-sided Lipschitz assumptions and an online extension. Relevant to continuous quote geometry under additional smoothness/distributional assumptions.
- Xu, Wang, Chen, and Wang (arXiv 2025, revised 2026) is verified. It studies dynamic pricing with adversarially censored demands and optimistic derivative estimates; useful as censored-pricing background, not directly delayed-fill regret.
- Graf and Mastrolia (arXiv:2601.17247, 2026) is verified. It studies learning market making with closing auctions via deep Q-learning and stochastic market models. This is recent adjacent market-making background only; it is not a delayed-fill tail characterization source.
- Jiang, Yang, Wang, Li, Huang, and Li (arXiv:2505.12465, 2025) is verified. It studies latency and inventory risk in reinforcement-learning market making. Useful only as adjacent latency/MM context, not as regret characterization.
- Xue, Du, and Xu (AAAI 2025) is verified. It studies online-learning market making with inventory constraints and fixed-spread reference strategies. Relevant background for market-making regret with state constraints, but not for delayed-fill tails.
- Dugast, Marta, and Riva (SSRN 2026) remains a candidate needing full verification. The report describes an execution-delay/order-book equilibrium paper; useful economics background only if confirmed.
- Maran and Restelli (arXiv:2605.19584, COLT 2026 accepted-papers page) is verified. It studies online market making with action-dependent order-book feedback and regret guarantees. Adjacent terminology and feedback-structure contrast only; it is not the same delayed-fill tail problem.
- Roesler and Szentes (AER 2017), Shen-Tang-Zeng (AAMAS 2018), and Condorelli-Szentes (JPE 2020) are verified equal-revenue/monopoly-pricing sources. They support the \(1/e\) posted-price surplus geometry used in the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) dual, but they do not prove market-making lower bounds.
- Hartline's *Mechanism Design and Approximation* notes are useful background for equal-revenue distributions and monopoly pricing. Treat as pedagogical support rather than a primary theorem source for this run.
- Iteration 7 did not find a direct source for the exact finite-positive-support constant \(((m-1)/m)^m\). Treat CL-023/LB-018 as an internal AM-GM theorem unless a later audit finds a precise prior statement.
- Bergemann, Brooks, and Morris (AER 2015) is verified. It is foundational for price discrimination and surplus-frontier geometry behind the welfare-minus-posted-revenue viewpoint, but not a delayed market-making theorem.
- Bergemann and Schlag (JEEA 2008) is verified. It studies minimax/regret pricing without priors and is relevant robust-pricing background for primal action-law ideas, not a proof of CL-023.
- Bergemann and Schlag (Journal of Economic Theory 2011), "Robust Monopoly Pricing," is verified. It studies maximin and minimax-regret monopoly pricing under model uncertainty. Relevant as adjacent robust-pricing context for randomized primal action laws, not as a source for the exact finite-\(C\) root formula.
- Chen, Hu, and Wang (Operations Research 2024) is verified. It gives robust screening/minimax-pricing duality adjacent to the \(\kappa\) LP viewpoint.
- Caldentey, Liu, and Lobel (Operations Research 2017), "Intertemporal Pricing Under Minimax Regret," is verified. Relevant dynamic minimax-pricing background for known-path anytime upper-bound attempts, not a delayed-fill theorem.
- Cummings, Devanur, Huang, and Wang (SODA 2020) is verified. It provides finite-dimensional algorithmic price-discrimination/segmentation LP context that may be useful for computational versions of \(\kappa\).
- Ko and Munagala (EC 2022; ACM TEAC 2024) is verified. It uses finite equal-revenue signal constructions in price discrimination; useful adjacent evidence for finite equal-revenue geometry, not a direct citation for the finite-\(m\) theorem.
- Kocyigit, Rujeerapaiboon, and Kuhn (Mathematical Programming 2022), "Robust Multidimensional Pricing: Separation Without Regret," is verified. It gives adjacent minimax-regret pricing and finite/discrete approximation context; it is not a source for the compact suffix-menu theorem.
- Wang, Liu, and Zhang (Manufacturing & Service Operations Management 2024), "Minimax Regret Robust Screening with Moment Information," is verified. It uses finite-support LP formulations in robust screening; useful as an analogy for compact-menu approximation, not direct proof support.
- Xie, Zhu, and Shishkin (Theoretical Economics 2025), "On the limitations of data-based price discrimination," is verified. It is adjacent data-driven price discrimination and robust/minimax pricing context; useful for pricing/equal-revenue background only, not a delayed-fill theorem.

## Quantifiers, Universal Regret, And Minimax

- Cesa-Bianchi and Lugosi, *Prediction, Learning, and Games* (Cambridge University Press, 2006), verified from the authors' official book page. It is useful for standard online-learning quantifiers: algorithms working for all individual sequences relative to a reference class, expert advice, and minimax regret.
- Yao (FOCS 1977) is verified through DOI/IEEE/DBLP metadata. It is relevant background for distributional-vs-randomized lower-bound framing, although the finite two-continuation block proof here is elementary averaging and does not require invoking the full principle.
- Cesa-Bianchi, Gentile, Mansour, and Minora (COLT/PMLR 2016) is now verified. The source explicitly mentions a minimax characterization for bandit learning with delay as a by-product. Relevant lower-bound methodology/background for class-uniform delayed-feedback impossibility, not direct support for the market-making conjecture.
- Abernethy, Bartlett, and Hazan (COLT/PMLR 2011) is verified. Approachability/no-regret equivalence may help if simultaneous-prefix constraints are formulated as feasibility/approachability, but it does not prove the horizon-oblivious lower bound.
- Hazan and Seshadhri (ICML/ACM 2009) and Daniely, Gonen, and Shalev-Shwartz (ICML/PMLR 2015) are verified adaptive/strongly-adaptive regret references. They are useful contrast for simultaneous interval/prefix guarantees, but their ordinary per-round loss setting does not contradict horizon-censored quote rewards.
- Luo and Schapire (ICML/PMLR 2014) is verified. It is useful for fixed-horizon versus unknown/adversarial-horizon online-learning terminology; it does not prove or refute the horizon-censored market-making obstruction.
- Gofer and Mansour (Machine Learning 2016) is verified. It lower-bounds individual-sequence anytime regret for broad online linear optimization forecasters. Relevant background for anytime-prefix lower bounds, but the market-making prefix conflict is a separate direct argument.
- Guzman, Mehta, and Mortazavi (NeurIPS 2021) is verified. It studies best-case lower bounds and adaptivity in online learning. It is conceptual background for avoiding quantifier confusion, not direct proof support.
- Mannor, Tsitsiklis, and Yu (JMLR 2009) is verified. Its sample-path constraint framework may be useful if prefix compatibility is recast as a constrained online-learning feasibility problem, but this remains speculative.
- Lagziel and Lehrer (Journal of Economic Theory 2015), "Approachability with delayed information," is verified. It gives delayed-information approachability/no-regret background and is relevant to vector-deficit formulations of simultaneous-horizon constraints; it does not prove the known-path finite-prefix LP or compactness criterion.
- Blackwell (Pacific J. Math. 1956) and Sion (Pacific J. Math. 1958) are verified as classical approachability/minimax references. They are relevant only as conceptual/minimax support for the prefix-compatibility LP; the market-making transfer still needs a direct proof.
- Kwon and Perchet (AISTATS/PMLR 2017) and Kwon (JMLR 2021) are verified. They provide modern approachability/partial-monitoring/global-cost background for vector-deficit formulations, but do not directly prove the proposed \(\kappa\) modulus theorem.
- Luo, Senapati, and Sharan (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2025) is verified. Its simultaneous-regret/calibration viewpoint is a loose analogy for simultaneous-prefix compatibility, not a delayed-feedback or market-making result.
- Hu, Tian, and Yang (COLT 2026/arXiv:2602.17577) is verified. It develops simultaneous Blackwell approachability with coupled actions for multiclass omniprediction; useful analogy for simultaneous-prefix feasibility, not direct support for the delayed market-making lower bounds.
- Frongillo, Luo, Mehta, and Schneider (COLT 2026/arXiv:2606.18527) is verified. It studies simultaneous regret in U-calibration; useful analogy for one forecast/action sequence serving many downstream losses, but not a delayed-feedback theorem.
- Anagnostides, Farina, Fishelson, Luo, and Schneider (arXiv:2602.06264, 2026) is verified. It uses response-based approachability for swap-regret minimization. Weak analogy only for future vector-payoff/simultaneous-regret formulations; the iteration-9 \(1/e\) upper bound is elementary and does not need this source.
- Kleinberg, Paes Leme, Schneider, and Teng (COLT/PMLR 2023) is verified. It introduces U-calibration as regret against all bounded scoring rules for an unknown downstream agent. Useful analogy for one sequence serving many downstream objectives, similar in spirit to one quote sequence serving many horizons.
- Hu and Wu (FOCS 2024/arXiv:2404.13503) is verified. Its Calibration Decision Loss measures worst-case downstream payoff improvement from recalibration. Useful terminology for decision-relevant error/loss replacing raw tail metrics.
- Luo, Senapati, and Sharan (NeurIPS/OpenReview 2024/arXiv:2405.19374) is verified. It gives minimax rates for multiclass U-calibration, reinforcing simultaneous-regret analogies; not direct support for delayed market making.
- Bairaktari, Hu, Nguyen, and Ullman (arXiv:2605.17749, 2026) is verified as a recent calibration candidate. It emphasizes measures that are both actionable for swap regret and testable. Useful only as an analogy for actionable delay-tail or information-loss statistics.
- Mannor, Perchet, and Stoltz (JMLR 2014) is verified. It studies set-valued approachability and partial monitoring; relevant as vector/set-valued feasibility background for horizon-indexed deficits, not a market-making theorem.
- Lehrer (International Journal of Game Theory 2002/2003) and Lehrer (Games and Economic Behavior 2003) are verified. They give infinite-dimensional approachability and wide-range no-regret background, respectively; useful analogies for one strategy serving many criteria.
- Bernstein and Shimkin (JMLR 2015) is verified. Response-based approachability may be algorithmically relevant if finite-prefix dual constraints are recast as vector-deficit feasibility; no direct delayed-fill result.
- Dann, Mansour, Mohri, Schneider, and Sivan (COLT/PMLR 2025) is verified. It discusses rate-preserving reductions between approachability and generalized regret; relevant background for any future approachability formulation, not proof support for \(D_n\)-gluing.
- Uniform-value sources from dynamic programming and games, especially Mertens-Neyman 1981, Renault 2011, and Li-Venel 2016, are verified. They support the caution that finite-horizon value behavior often needs additional stability/total-boundedness/nonexpansiveness assumptions to yield one uniform strategy.
- Flesch, Laraki, and Perchet (COLT/PMLR 2016) is verified as a two-page quitting-games approachability source. It is weak background for fixed-horizon versus anytime approachability distinctions and should not be cited as direct support without a sharper theorem.
- Renault (Journal of Dynamics and Games 2014), "General limit value in dynamic programming," is verified. It gives a total-boundedness criterion for uniform convergence of values under vanishing-impatience evaluations. Relevant as uniform-value/gluing background only; it is not a theorem about delayed market making or \(D_n\).
- Dann, Mansour, Mohri, Schneider, and Sivan (ALT/PMLR 2023), "Pseudonorm Approachability and Applications to Regret Minimization," is verified. It develops \(\ell_\infty\)/pseudonorm approachability tools for many regret constraints. Useful if horizon-indexed deficits are recast as approachability constraints; not direct proof support.
- Rakhlin, Sridharan, and Tewari (COLT/PMLR 2011), "Online Learning: Beyond Regret," is verified. It is conceptual background for global/non-additive online-learning objectives; not a market-specific gluing theorem.
- Wu, Heidari, Grama, and Szpankowski (IEEE ISIT 2022), "Sequential vs. Fixed Design Regrets in Online Learning," is verified. It is useful terminology for fixed-design/path-known versus sequential/universal regret, matching this run's quantifier distinctions.
- Assos, Attias, Dagan, Daskalakis, and Fishelson (COLT/PMLR 2023), "Online Learning and Solving Infinite Games with an ERM Oracle," is verified. It is possible background for best-response-oracle or infinite-game formulations of the fixed-envelope criterion; not a delayed-fill theorem.
- Wu, Grama, and Szpankowski (COLT/PMLR 2023), "Online Learning in Dynamically Changing Environments," is verified. Its process-complexity viewpoint may be useful if finite-alphabet paths are parameterized by switching or drought complexity; not direct proof support.
- Kontonis, Ma, and Tzamos (NeurIPS 2023), "The Gain from Ordering in Online Learning," is verified. It studies fixed-design/self-directed online learning and is an analogy for known-path order effects only.
- Rakhlin and Sridharan (COLT/PMLR 2013), "Online Learning with Predictable Sequences," is verified. It is background for predictable-path/optimistic methods and stable-policy regimes; not a characterization of delayed market-making tails.

## Finite LP, Interval, And Approximation Background

- Fulkerson and Gross (Pacific Journal of Mathematics 1965), "Incidence matrices and interval graphs," is verified. It is relevant background for consecutive-ones/interval-incidence structure in the finite-alphabet dual, but it does not imply the desired uncrossing/localization theorem because the market dual has nested maxima.
- Kuntz, Thomas, Stan, and Barahona (SIAM Journal on Optimization 2021), "Approximations of Countably Infinite Linear Programs over Bounded Measure Spaces," is verified. It may provide language for countably infinite LP approximation and bounded measure-space feasibility, but any application to the fixed-envelope criterion remains speculative.
- Lindley (Mathematical Proceedings of the Cambridge Philosophical Society 1952), "The theory of queues with a single server," is verified. It is useful only as background for possible reflected-surplus/debt recursions in two-level drought bookkeeping; it is not a market-making result.

## Composite Or Anonymous Feedback

- Cesa-Bianchi, Gentile, and Mansour (COLT/PMLR 2018), verified, studies composite anonymous feedback where losses are spread over bounded windows and only aggregates are observed. This is harder/different than the public-price model, so use only as comparison.
- Pike-Burke, Agrawal, Szepesvari, and Grunewalder (ICML/PMLR 2018) is now verified. It studies delayed, aggregated anonymous bandit feedback; relevant background for distinguishing identifiable public fills from harder anonymous feedback, but not direct proof support.

## Probabilistic Method And Empirical-Process Background

- Hoeffding (JASA 1963) is verified and is the direct concentration source for the iteration-10 grid-union-bound derandomization lemma. The proof only needs the standard bounded independent-sum tail inequality.
- Alon and Spencer, *The Probabilistic Method* (Wiley, 4th ed. 2016), is verified as general background for proving existence by a random construction, union bounds, and Borel-Cantelli. It is not a delayed-market-making source.
- Van der Vaart and Wellner, *Weak Convergence and Empirical Processes* (Springer, 1996), is verified and provides empirical-process/bracketing background. It is useful if the monotone-threshold lemma is later written via bracketing rather than the elementary grid proof.
- Gao and Wellner (JMVA 2007/arXiv:math/0512641) is verified. It gives monotone-function entropy context and cautions about high-dimensional monotone classes; it is not needed for the accepted \(O(n^{2/3}\sqrt{\log n})\) grid proof.
- Niederreiter (SIAM, 1992) is verified as a quasi-Monte Carlo/low-discrepancy reference. It is relevant only if a future iteration tries to replace the nonconstructive deterministic sequence by an explicit construction.
- Vapnik and Chervonenkis (Theory of Probability and Its Applications 1971) is verified as classical uniform-convergence background. For the iteration-10 monotone-boundary class, a naive finite-VC route is not the preferred proof and should not be cited as direct support.

## Current Takeaway

Existing delayed-learning literature supports sufficiency via bounded, total, adaptive, optimistic, skipped, outstanding-observation, or intermediate-observation delay terms. It does not support necessity of the unweighted \(\beta\) tail. Standard online-learning references also reinforce that the meaningful lower-bound regime is usually universal/minimax or simultaneous-prefix, not horizon- and law-specific oracle policies. Iteration 4's accepted concatenation theorems are direct elementary lower bounds, with the cited unknown-horizon/anytime literature serving as terminology and contrast. The literature is more aligned with aggregate, quantile, best-arm, skipped, value-of-information, horizon-censored, censored-threshold, or adaptive-regret quantities, but the counterexamples show raw delayed reward mass is not necessary for instance-wise learnability unless tied to unpredictability, prefix incompatibility, or information acquisition.

Iteration 6 verifies that the multi-threshold \(\kappa\) LP aligns with classical posted-price/equal-revenue geometry: local gaps are welfare minus monopoly revenue and are capped by \(1/e\). The simultaneous-approachability and U-calibration sources are analogies for one action sequence satisfying several objectives. None of these external sources supplies a global delayed market-making characterization; the accepted lower bounds remain internal prefix-indistinguishability arguments.

Iteration 7 sharpens the internal threshold geometry: the exact \(m\)-positive-support local gap is \(((m-1)/m)^m\), approaching \(1/e\). The new price-discrimination/robust-pricing sources above are useful context for equal-revenue and minimax duality, but the finite-support constant and the terminal-block \(1/e\) universal lift should currently be cited as internal results, not as imported theorems.

Iteration 8 promotes the same-path multi-threshold anytime lift internally; no direct external theorem was found for this delayed-market-making statement. The new calibration/U-calibration sources are decision-relevant simultaneous-objective analogies only. They should be cited, if at all, to motivate replacing raw tail conditions by payoff/actionability moduli, not as proof support for CL-026/CL-027.

Iteration 9 adds two internal results: the exact finite-\(C\) local root formula \(\kappa(C)=\delta_*(C)\), and the universal equal-revenue \(1/e\) upper bound for exogenous horizon-oblivious paths. A narrow literature check found robust/minimax-pricing analogies but no direct source for the exact root formula or the delayed-market-making \(1/e\) theorem. Treat Bergemann-Schlag 2011 and Caldentey-Liu-Lobel 2017 as adjacent context only, and Anagnostides et al. 2026 as a weak approachability analogy only.

Iteration 10 adds an internal deterministic derandomization of the known-path \(1/e\) upper bound. The external literature supports only the proof technology: Hoeffding for concentration, probabilistic-method references for nonconstructive existence, and empirical-process/bracketing sources for optional rate improvements. No direct delayed-market-making theorem was found for this derandomization, and the result remains separate from the false original tail-characterization conjecture.

Iteration 11 adds the compact suffix-menu local formula and the resulting path-aware known-path sufficient condition. The robust/minimax-pricing sources above remain adjacent analogies for randomized pricing, finite approximation, and LP/minimax structure; no external source found in the iteration reports proves the compact strict-crossing menu formula or the delayed-market-making suffix theorem. The alternating-path stress test confirms the suffix-menu condition is sufficient only.

Iteration 12 adds internal exact finite-prefix machinery for deterministic known exogenous paths: the marginal reduction, finite-prefix LP dual \(D_n(P)\), and fixed-envelope compactness criterion. Literature checks found no direct theorem for this delayed-fill characterization. Lagziel-Lehrer 2015 is relevant delayed-approachability background for future vector-deficit formulations; Bistritz et al. 2022 is relevant weighted-regret delayed-bandit background for horizon-weighted certificates; Xie-Zhu-Shishkin 2025 is adjacent pricing context. All three should be cited as background only.

Iteration 13 found no external theorem proving \(D_n=o(n)\Rightarrow o(T)\), nor a known market-path separator from the fixed-envelope criterion. The added approachability and uniform-value sources are background for possible formulations and for gluing cautions. Current internal proof work should focus on the last-exceedance/finite-prefix dual structure rather than importing uniform-value theorems wholesale.

Iteration 14 found no direct external theorem proving gluing or a separator. The internal progress is an exact separated-block value, an exact dual boundary identity, and a finite-alphabet dual reduction. Fulkerson-Gross supports the interval-incidence vocabulary for the finite-alphabet formula, Renault 2014 and existing uniform-value sources reinforce the need for stability beyond scalar finite-prefix values, and pseudonorm/approachability and countably-infinite LP sources are possible formulation tools only.

