. Choose `H(T)` so both are `o(T)`.\n\nThat is a solid proof strategy.\n\nThe main caveats are:\n\n- The bound\n  ```tex\n  R(T) - R^H(T) \\leq T\\beta(H(T))\n  ```\n  is not literally true for the realized regret as currently defined, because `R(T)` has a `max_a` over random realized rewards. The `T\\beta(H)` argument works cleanly for expected/pseudo-regret:\n  ```tex\n  \\max_a \\mathbb{E}\\sum_t r_t(a) - \\mathbb{E}\\sum_t r_t(a_t).\n  ```\n  For realized regret, you need concentration or a stronger tail condition.\n\n- The assumption should probably be conditional on history. Something like:\n  ```tex\n  \\beta(h)\n  =\n  \\sup_t \\sup_{p_1,\\dots,p_t} \\sup_a\n  \\Pr(h < \\tau_t(a) < \\infty \\mid P_1=p_1,\\dots,P_t=p_t).\n  ```\n  Otherwise an adaptive learner could land in histories where the delay tail is worse than the unconditional one.\n\n- The discretization step is plausible, but it needs to be stated. Here it should work nicely because if you round a quote `a` down to a grid point within `1/K`, the reward drops by at most `1/K` per round. So you get a discretization cost like `T/K`.\n\n- The “only if” direction is much less clear. In fact, the current assumption may be too strong to be necessary. For example, if the bad long-delay behavior only occurs for tiny quotes with tiny reward, then `\\sup_a \\beta(a,h,t)` might fail to vanish even though regret is still learnable.\n\nSo my diagnosis: the if-direction sketch is basically right after changing the claim to expected regret and tightening the tail assumption. The iff conjecture is still suspicious; I’d first prove a clean sufficient-condition theorem, then think separately about the right necessary condition."}
