ential supremum is the biggest value a random variable gets **after ignoring probability-zero weirdness**.\n\nExample: let `X` be uniform on `[0,1]`, and define\n\n```tex\nf(x) =\n\\begin{cases}\n100 & x = 1/2,\\\\\n1 & \\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n```\n\nThe ordinary supremum is:\n\n```tex\n\\sup_x f(x) = 100\n```\n\nbecause there is one point where the function equals `100`.\n\nBut the essential supremum is:\n\n```tex\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup} f(X) = 1\n```\n\nbecause the event `X = 1/2` has probability zero, so probabilistically that spike never matters.\n\nIn our case,\n\n```tex\n\\Pr[h < \\tau_t(a) < \\infty \\mid \\mathcal{F}_t]\n```\n\nis a random variable depending on the history `P_1,\\dots,P_t`. Conditional probabilities are only defined up to changes on probability-zero histories. So taking an ordinary supremum over all histories is not well-defined unless we choose a specific version of the conditional probability.\n\nThe essential supremum avoids this by saying: ignore histories that occur with probability zero, and take the worst case over the rest.\n\nSo morally:\n\n```tex\n\\sup = worst case over every possible point\n```\n\nwhile\n\n```tex\n\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup} = worst case over points that actually matter probabilistically\n```"}
