u did not say essential supremum over what? is that not necessary?\n","ok i like that second version, let's use that\n","ok now what did you say about regret vs expected regret?\n"],"last-assistant-message":"Right now the draft defines realized regret:\n\n```tex\nR(T)\n=\n\\max_{a \\in [0,1]} \\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\n-\n\\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a_t).\n```\n\nThis is a random quantity depending on the realized price path.\n\nBut the proof sketch later uses a tail-probability argument:\n\n```tex\nR(T) - R^H(T) \\leq T\\beta(H(T)).\n```\n\nThat kind of bound is naturally true in **expectation**, not pathwise. Why? Because `\\beta(H)` controls the probability that a reward arrives after the truncation horizon. It does not prevent a particular realized path from having many long-delayed trades; it only says those are unlikely.\n\nSo the cleaner object for the proof is expected regret, something like:\n\n```tex\n\\mathcal{R}(T)\n=\n\\max_{a \\in [0,1]} \\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a)\\right]\n-\n\\mathbb{E}\\left[\\sum_{t=1}^T r_t(a_t)\\right].\n```\n\nThen the truncation comparison works:\n\n```tex\n\\mathbb{E}[r_t(a) - r^H_t(a)]\n=\na \\Pr(H < \\tau_t(a) \\le T-t)\n\\le\n\\beta(H),\n```\n\nuniformly over `a,t`, so the total loss from truncation is at most about `T\\beta(H)`.\n\nThere is one more subtlety: for the learner’s chosen actions `a_t`, truncation only decreases reward, so\n\n```tex\nr_t(a_t) \\ge r^H_t(a_t).\n```\n\nThe main comparison is really between the best fixed action in the true problem and the best fixed action in the truncated problem:\n\n```tex\n\\max_a \\mathbb{E}\\sum_t r_t(a)\n\\le\n\\max_a \\mathbb{E}\\sum_t r^H_t(a)\n+\nT\\beta(H).\n```\n\nThat gives:\n\n```tex\n\\mathcal{R}(T)\n\\le\n\\mathcal{R}^H(T) + T\\beta(H).\n```\n\nFor realized regret, you’d need concentration/martingale arguments to say the number of long-delayed rewards is usually close to its expectation. The current assumption alone is enough for expected regret, but not obviously enough for high-probability or pathwise regret."}
