"compile\n","compile\n","ok can you give me comments on the proof sketch i have written compared to the proof in [game_tree_imprecise_bandits_learning.tex](game_tree_imprecise_bandits_learning.tex) again? of course this is only a proof sketch, so i am skipping *lots* of details. but i want to understand if i got the basic crux of the idea right. \n"],"last-assistant-message":"Yes. I think you have the basic crux right.\n\nThe core idea in your sketch matches the proof in [game_tree_imprecise_bandits_learning.tex](/Users/vinayakpathak/Documents/efficient_infrabayes/game_tree_imprecise_bandits_learning.tex:306):\n\n- Treat unknown learner nodes optimistically as reward \\(1\\).\n- Plan optimally on the truncated/optimistic tree.\n- If player 2 avoids unknown nodes, then those unknown nodes do not hurt realized reward.\n- If player 2 routes into an unknown node, the learner may pay regret for that episode, but gets useful data.\n- Therefore one-step regret is roughly\n  \\[\n  O(H\\epsilon) + \\Pr(\\text{hit unknown node}),\n  \\]\n  and the total number of unknown-node hits is bounded by the sample budget.\n- Balancing \\(NH\\epsilon\\) against the exploration budget \\(\\sim 1/\\epsilon^2\\) gives the \\(N^{2/3}\\)-type rate.\n\nThat is absolutely the heart of the proof.\n\nThe main places where the sketch is slightly too compressed are:\n\n1. In [proof_sketch.tex](/Users/vinayakpathak/Documents/efficient_infrabayes/proof_sketch.tex:18), “node visited \\(D/\\epsilon^2\\) times” hides an important point. The full proof needs each basis arm \\(b_{s,j}\\) at node \\(s\\) sampled enough times. A raw count of visits to \\(s\\) is not enough unless the algorithm controls which arms were played there.\n\n2. In [proof_sketch.tex](/Users/vinayakpathak/Documents/efficient_infrabayes/proof_sketch.tex:22), saying the learner estimates \\(\\theta_s\\) is fine intuition, but the actual proof estimates the transition distributions \\(p_{\\theta,s}(\\cdot\\mid b_{s,j})\\), then extends to all arms using the barycentric spanner. That avoids needing identifiability of \\(\\theta_s\\) itself.\n\n3. In [proof_sketch.tex](/Users/vinayakpathak/Documents/efficient_infrabayes/proof_sketch.tex:24), \\(T'\\) is best thought of as the truncated optimistic tree: when play first reaches an unknown learner node, stop and pay reward \\(1\\). It is not literally that all unknown subtrees become known.\n\n4. In [proof_sketch.tex](/Users/vinayakpathak/Documents/efficient_infrabayes/proof_sketch.tex:32), the bound on \\(\\OPT(T')-\\pi_t(T')\\) comes from two ingredients together: uniform local estimation on known nodes and backward induction through the tree. Claim 1 is gesturing at this, but that is the real mechanism.\n\n5. The full proof uses conditional expectations:\n   \\[\n   \\Pr(E_t\\mid \\mathcal F_{t-1})\n   \\]\n   rather than just \\(\\Pr(E_t)\\). Your version is fine for a sketch after taking expectations, but the conditional form is what handles adaptive player-2 behavior cleanly.\n\nSo my verdict: the sketch gets the conceptual engine right. The one thing I would sharpen, even in a sketch, is the definition of “known”: not “visited enough times” but “sampled enough times on the exploration basis arms.” That is the piece that makes the statistical claim actually true."}
